{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787397884958,"sessionID":"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"toolu_016b2TiKzvfN7WvBmqivi2NK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 && cat .model-key; echo ---; cat worker.log"},"output":"copilot-sonnet5\n---\n\n[ringer.py] attempt 1 started 2026-08-22T11:24:16.919288+00:00\n[ringer.py] engine: opencode\n[ringer.py] command: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/dev/ringer/engines/opencode-sandboxed.sh /private/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 run -m github-copilot/claude-sonnet-5 --dangerously-skip-permissions --format json --dir /private/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 'You are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain from its specification alone, as a clean-room exercise. Your working directory contains PACKET.md and a cases/ directory. Read PACKET.md in full before starting: it carries the language specification you must implement against, the hard rules, and the exact output contract you are marked on. BOUNDARY: you own only your own working directory. Do NOT read, search for, or open any file outside it — a reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine and is off limits, as is any directory of design notes. Do not modify anything under cases/. DELIVERABLE: an executable file named switchcheck in your working directory, runnable as ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>, with a shebang so it runs directly. Any language available on the machine is fine; no network access, no package installs. OUTPUT CONTRACT: for the file it is given, switchcheck prints one lowercase diagnostic tag per line on stdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed program prints NOTHING. The permitted tags and their meanings are in PACKET.md. HOW YOU ARE MARKED: your switchcheck is run over the cases and its tags compared against the reference compiler'\"'\"'s for the same file; every case must match exactly. Printing a diagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not guess defensively — a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails the very first case. Test your implementation against the files in cases/ before you finish.' < /dev/null\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397860961,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936ee5e001jLTAlJT0E1x1Kw\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936e78b001S6ppz4WCPQ1vaP\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397861904,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01LqyWjQsz2SMcLE4375oFgt\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 && ls -la && wc -l PACKET.md && ls cases | head -50\"},\"output\":\"total 48\\ndrwxr-xr-x   6 davidwilliams  wheel    192 22 Aug 12:24 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x   7 davidwilliams  wheel    224 22 Aug 12:23 ..\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel     16 22 Aug 12:23 .model-key\\ndrwxr-xr-x  10 davidwilliams  wheel    320 22 Aug 12:23 cases\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel  13277 22 Aug 12:23 PACKET.md\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel   2222 22 Aug 12:24 worker.log\\n     305 PACKET.md\\nc01-exhaustive-closed\\nc02-catchall-open\\nc03-inexhaustive\\nc04-unreachable\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive\\nc07-guarded\\nc08-wrong-return\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"total 48\\ndrwxr-xr-x   6 davidwilliams  wheel    192 22 Aug 12:24 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x   7 davidwilliams  wheel    224 22 Aug 12:23 ..\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel     16 22 Aug 12:23 .model-key\\ndrwxr-xr-x  10 davidwilliams  wheel    320 22 Aug 12:23 cases\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel  13277 22 Aug 12:23 PACKET.md\\n-rw-r--r--   1 davidwilliams  wheel   2222 22 Aug 12:24 worker.log\\n     305 PACKET.md\\nc01-exhaustive-closed\\nc02-catchall-open\\nc03-inexhaustive\\nc04-unreachable\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive\\nc07-guarded\\nc08-wrong-return\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 && ls -la && wc -l PACKET.md && ls cases | head -50\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397861885,\"end\":1787397861888}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_02936f097001QH1Le7VZZKU64q\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936e78b001S6ppz4WCPQ1vaP\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397861904,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936f202001xptE173crDkmJQ\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936e78b001S6ppz4WCPQ1vaP\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":45575,\"input\":2,\"output\":108,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":45465,\"read\":0}},\"cost\":0.1147465}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397863017,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936f662001M7c4VbbP8nZzq6\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f2050018Zeh2JlP4ArhUL\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397863453,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01R455gQkTG9FKLp3cNo3iaW\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\\n2: \\n3: You are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\\n4: SPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\\n5: exists, and you must not look at it.\\n6: \\n7: ## Hard rules\\n8: \\n9: - **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\\n10:   A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\\n11:   as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\\n12:   yourself looking for either, stop.\\n13: - Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\\n14: - Work only inside your own working directory.\\n15: - Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\\n16: \\n17: ## What you are building\\n18: \\n19: A command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\\n20: reports the problems it finds.\\n21: \\n22: Deliverable: an executable file named `switchcheck` in your working directory:\\n23: \\n24:     ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>\\n25: \\n26: Any language available on the machine is fine, provided `./switchcheck` runs\\n27: directly via a shebang. No network access, no package installs.\\n28: \\n29: ## Output contract\\n30: \\n31: For the file it is given, `switchcheck` prints **one lowercase tag per line** on\\n32: stdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed\\n33: program prints **nothing at all**.\\n34: \\n35: These are the only tags you may print:\\n36: \\n37:     switch_inexhaustive\\n38:     unreachable_arm\\n39:     rebinding\\n40:     return_not_declared\\n41: \\n42: **What each of those means is for you to determine from the specification\\n43: below.** The names are listed so that your output can be compared\\n44: mechanically — they are a vocabulary, not a definition of the rules.\\n45: \\n46: Exit code is ignored. Order does not matter and duplicates are ignored; only\\n47: the set of tags is compared.\\n48: \\n49: ## How you are marked\\n50: \\n51: Your `switchcheck` is run over the files in `cases/` and the tags it prints are\\n52: compared against the tags the reference compiler produces for the same file.\\n53: **Every case must match exactly.**\\n54: \\n55: Some of the files are well-formed and must produce no output at all. Printing a\\n56: diagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not\\n57: guess defensively: a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails\\n58: on the first case. Test against `cases/` before you finish.\\n59: \\n60: ## The specification\\n61: \\n62: What follows is the relevant part of the language reference, verbatim. It is all\\n63: you get, and it is meant to be enough. Where it is not enough, prefer the\\n64: reading the text best supports rather than inventing a rule.\\n65: \\n66: ---\\n67: \\n68: ## 2. Multi-clause heads\\n69: \\n70: The one structural move the language rests on: C#'s pattern grammar moves out of `switch` arms and\\n71: into the **parameter position**, and N declarations are allowed where C# allows one.\\n72: \\n73: <!-- check:\\n74: type Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\\n75: -->\\n76: ```csharp\\n77: type Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\\n78: \\n79: public Verdict Classify(Reading r)\\n80: \\n81: Classify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\\n82: Classify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\\n83: Classify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\\n84: Classify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\\n85: ```\\n86: \\n87: Five clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out. **shipped**\\n88: \\n89: **The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*\\n90: input type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,\\n91: because the answer is always yes. **shipped**\\n92: \\n93: **Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation\\n94: refines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**\\n95: \\n96: ```csharp\\n97: Classify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\\n98: Classify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\\n99: Classify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\\n100: ```\\n101: \\n102: That is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer\\n103: intervals.\\n104: \\n105: **One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and\\n106: no `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the\\n107: *parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.\\n108: C# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because\\n109: patterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.\\n110: \\n111: **A span of integers is a relational pattern.** `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a half-open\\n112: slice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position already means \\\"the rest\\\" — which\\n113: this language uses for lists. **shipped**\\n114: \\n115: <!-- check:\\n116: public atom Classify(int n)\\n117: -->\\n118: ```csharp\\n119: Classify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\\n120: Classify(<= -1)         -> :negative\\n121: Classify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\\n122: Classify(>= 8)          -> :high\\n123: ```\\n124: \\n125: Those four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\\n126: looking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\\n127: whole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\\n128: \\n129: The rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\\n130: the glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\\n131: and costs a false friend.\\n132: \\n133: **A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\\n134: is two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\\n135: marker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\\n136: is and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\\n137: <!-- ticket 08 as amended by ticket 54; ticket 53 found the closed form and\\n138:      ticket 54 replaced its spelling; the four-language survey is in 54 -->\\n139: \\n140: `[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\\n141: language's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\\n142: — and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\\n143: \\n144: <!-- check:\\n145: public atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\\n146: -->\\n147: ```csharp\\n148: Dispatch([\\\"orders\\\"])     -> :index\\n149: Dispatch([\\\"orders\\\", id]) -> :show\\n150: Dispatch(_)              -> :not_found\\n151: ```\\n152: \\n153: That is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\\n154: `/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\\n155: \\n156: **The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\\n157: product of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\\n158: so length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\\n159: clause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\\n160: an O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\\n161: bounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\\n162: recursion terminate. **shipped**\\n163: <!-- ticket 54, built as F20; the repro it deletes is four lines and is in that ticket -->\\n164: \\n165: A consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\\n166: closed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\\n167: `[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\\n168: `list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\\n169: \\n170: **To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\\n171: introduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\\n172: \\n173: So a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\\n174: both are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\\n175: acc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\\n176: language has no way to say *the same value again*.\\n177: \\n178: ```csharp\\n179: public int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\\n180: \\n181: RunLength(head, [])                -> 0\\n182: RunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\\n183: RunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\\n184: ```\\n185: \\n186: It is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\\n187: head reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\\n188: `==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\\n189: second spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\\n190: \\n191: The space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\\n192: `>= 4`.\\n193: \\n194: This is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\\n195: and push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\\n196: a pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\\n197: guard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\\n198: credits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\\n199: what is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\\n200: name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\\n201: \\n202: ---\\n203: \\n204: ---\\n205: \\n206: ## 3. Exhaustiveness\\n207: \\n208: **A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\\n209: **residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\\n210: rather than a complaint to interpret.\\n211: \\n212: ```\\n213: readings.bs:4: error: Classify is not exhaustive\\n214:   no clause matches:\\n215:     Classify((:ok, int <= 0)) -> ...\\n216: ```\\n217: \\n218: The error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\\n219: as a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\\n220: how many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\\n221: **shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\\n222: \\n223: A **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\\n224: unbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\\n225: the language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **decided**\\n226: \\n227: ---\\n228: \\n229: ---\\n230: \\n231: ## 5. Control flow\\n232: \\n233: **`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\\n234: <!-- decided by ticket 17, which also settled `|>` and `|?>` -->\\n235: \\n236: <!-- check:\\n237: type Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\\n238: record Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\\n239: -->\\n240: ```csharp\\n241: public Verdict Describe(Order o)\\n242: \\n243: Describe(o) -> o.Status switch {\\n244:     :placed  => :new,\\n245:     :shipped => :gone,\\n246:     _        => :unknown\\n247: }\\n248: ```\\n249: \\n250: The `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\\n251: cannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\\n252: residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\\n253: name it. **That rule is decided and is not yet enforced**, at a switch arm or at a clause head.\\n254: \\n255: For compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\\n256: level down:\\n257: \\n258: <!-- check:\\n259: type Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\\n260: -->\\n261: ```csharp\\n262: public Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\n263: \\n264: Decide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n265:     (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n266:     (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n267:     (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n268:     (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n269: }\\n270: ```\\n271: \\n272: Exhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\\n273: grammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\\n274: \\n275: ```csharp\\n276: public atom Classify(int n)\\n277: \\n278: Classify(n) -> n switch {\\n279:     m when m < 5 => :retried,\\n280:     >= 5         => :exhausted\\n281: }\\n282: ```\\n283: \\n284: **The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\\n285: a bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\\n286: the parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\\n287: A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\\n288: `< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\\n289: <!-- ticket 45 owns `== name`; ticket 34 owns rebinding. This paragraph exists because §5 used to\\n290:      illustrate the guard with `n when n < 5` beside a parameter named `n` — a program the compiler\\n291:      rejects — in loose prose that no fence gated. Three clean-room candidates read the packet, had\\n292:      §2's rule in front of them, and all three reproduced the illustration rather than the rule:\\n293:      handoff/audition-switch, round 1, 2026-08-22. An example outranks a stated rule when the two\\n294:      disagree. -->\\n295: \\n296: Nested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\\n297: built: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\\n298: \\n299: **shipped** — F7.\\n300: \\n301: `else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\\n302: a pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\\n303: so far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\\n304: \\n305: ---\\n\\n(End of file - total 305 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\\n\\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\\nexists, and you must not look at it.\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n\\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\\n\\n## What you are building\\n\\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\\nreports the problems it finds.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md\",\"text\":\"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\\n\\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\\nexists, and you must not look at it.\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n\\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\\n\\n## What you are building\\n\\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\\nreports the problems it finds.\\n\\nDeliverable: an executable file named `switchcheck` in your working directory:\\n\\n    ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>\\n\\nAny language available on the machine is fine, provided `./switchcheck` runs\\ndirectly via a shebang. No network access, no package installs.\\n\\n## Output contract\\n\\nFor the file it is given, `switchcheck` prints **one lowercase tag per line** on\\nstdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed\\nprogram prints **nothing at all**.\\n\\nThese are the only tags you may print:\\n\\n    switch_inexhaustive\\n    unreachable_arm\\n    rebinding\\n    return_not_declared\\n\\n**What each of those means is for you to determine from the specification\\nbelow.** The names are listed so that your output can be compared\\nmechanically — they are a vocabulary, not a definition of the rules.\\n\\nExit code is ignored. Order does not matter and duplicates are ignored; only\\nthe set of tags is compared.\\n\\n## How you are marked\\n\\nYour `switchcheck` is run over the files in `cases/` and the tags it prints are\\ncompared against the tags the reference compiler produces for the same file.\\n**Every case must match exactly.**\\n\\nSome of the files are well-formed and must produce no output at all. Printing a\\ndiagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not\\nguess defensively: a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails\\non the first case. Test against `cases/` before you finish.\\n\\n## The specification\\n\\nWhat follows is the relevant part of the language reference, verbatim. It is all\\nyou get, and it is meant to be enough. Where it is not enough, prefer the\\nreading the text best supports rather than inventing a rule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Multi-clause heads\\n\\nThe one structural move the language rests on: C#'s pattern grammar moves out of `switch` arms and\\ninto the **parameter position**, and N declarations are allowed where C# allows one.\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\\n-->\\n```csharp\\ntype Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\\n\\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\\n\\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\\n```\\n\\nFive clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out. **shipped**\\n\\n**The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*\\ninput type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,\\nbecause the answer is always yes. **shipped**\\n\\n**Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation\\nrefines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**\\n\\n```csharp\\nClassify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\\nClassify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\\nClassify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\\n```\\n\\nThat is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer\\nintervals.\\n\\n**One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and\\nno `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the\\n*parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.\\nC# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because\\npatterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.\\n\\n**A span of integers is a relational pattern.** `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a half-open\\nslice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position already means \\\"the rest\\\" — which\\nthis language uses for lists. **shipped**\\n\\n<!-- check:\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\n-->\\n```csharp\\nClassify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\\nClassify(<= -1)         -> :negative\\nClassify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\\nClassify(>= 8)          -> :high\\n```\\n\\nThose four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\\nlooking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\\nwhole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\\n\\nThe rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\\nthe glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\\nand costs a false friend.\\n\\n**A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\\nis two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\\nmarker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\\nis and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\\n<!-- ticket 08 as amended by ticket 54; ticket 53 found the closed form and\\n     ticket 54 replaced its spelling; the four-language survey is in 54 -->\\n\\n`[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\\nlanguage's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\\n— and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\\n\\n<!-- check:\\npublic atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\\n-->\\n```csharp\\nDispatch([\\\"orders\\\"])     -> :index\\nDispatch([\\\"orders\\\", id]) -> :show\\nDispatch(_)              -> :not_found\\n```\\n\\nThat is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\\n`/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\\n\\n**The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\\nproduct of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\\nso length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\\nclause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\\nan O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\\nbounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\\nrecursion terminate. **shipped**\\n<!-- ticket 54, built as F20; the repro it deletes is four lines and is in that ticket -->\\n\\nA consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\\nclosed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\\n`[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\\n`list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\\n\\n**To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\\nintroduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\\n\\nSo a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\\nboth are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\\nacc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\\nlanguage has no way to say *the same value again*.\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\\n\\nRunLength(head, [])                -> 0\\nRunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\\nRunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\\n```\\n\\nIt is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\\nhead reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\\n`==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\\nsecond spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\\n\\nThe space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\\n`>= 4`.\\n\\nThis is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\\nand push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\\na pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\\nguard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\\ncredits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\\nwhat is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\\nname is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\\n\\n---\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Exhaustiveness\\n\\n**A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\\n**residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\\nrather than a complaint to interpret.\\n\\n```\\nreadings.bs:4: error: Classify is not exhaustive\\n  no clause matches:\\n    Classify((:ok, int <= 0)) -> ...\\n```\\n\\nThe error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\\nas a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\\nhow many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\\n**shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\\n\\nA **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\\nunbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\\nthe language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **decided**\\n\\n---\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Control flow\\n\\n**`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\\n<!-- decided by ticket 17, which also settled `|>` and `|?>` -->\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\\nrecord Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\\n-->\\n```csharp\\npublic Verdict Describe(Order o)\\n\\nDescribe(o) -> o.Status switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\\ncannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\\nresidual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\\nname it. **That rule is decided and is not yet enforced**, at a switch arm or at a clause head.\\n\\nFor compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\\nlevel down:\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\\n-->\\n```csharp\\npublic Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\n\\nDecide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n```\\n\\nExhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\\ngrammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\n\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    m when m < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\\na bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\\nthe parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\\nA bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\\n`< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\\n<!-- ticket 45 owns `== name`; ticket 34 owns rebinding. This paragraph exists because §5 used to\\n     illustrate the guard with `n when n < 5` beside a parameter named `n` — a program the compiler\\n     rejects — in loose prose that no fence gated. Three clean-room candidates read the packet, had\\n     §2's rule in front of them, and all three reproduced the illustration rather than the rule:\\n     handoff/audition-switch, round 1, 2026-08-22. An example outranks a stated rule when the two\\n     disagree. -->\\n\\nNested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\\nbuilt: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\\n\\n**shipped** — F7.\\n\\n`else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\\na pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\\nso far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\\n\\n---\",\"lineStart\":1,\"lineEnd\":305,\"totalLines\":305,\"truncated\":false}},\"title\":\"tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397863423,\"end\":1787397863436}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_02936f666001Fvue5GPjdwhGJS\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f2050018Zeh2JlP4ArhUL\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397863453,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936f80e001eKr5SRTjZpYlON\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f2050018Zeh2JlP4ArhUL\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":45985,\"input\":2,\"output\":75,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":443,\"read\":45465}},\"cost\":0.0109545}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397864579,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936fc7f001NIOOKKHt6Cy8BQ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397865611,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01BD7vyA2mLihgunSrGRwssC\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nVerdict\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nOpen\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nMissing\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nShadowed\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nDecide\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nPartial\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nRetry\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nWrong\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nVerdict\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nOpen\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nMissing\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nShadowed\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nDecide\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nPartial\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nRetry\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nWrong\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397865588,\"end\":1787397865591}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_02936fe940017h7TCz3nMRD530\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397865611,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370079001Y6d5tTpeB6H97s\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":51710,\"input\":2,\"output\":114,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":5686,\"read\":45908}},\"cost\":0.0245406}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397866691,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_0293704ba001pZhqcFUe6DUgKq\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397867444,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01E4g5jkW9AYNCGrDJNRRc7K\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"*/ 2>/dev/null; find \\\"$d\\\" -type f; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc07-guarded/Retry/in.bs\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc07-guarded/Retry/in.bs\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"*/ 2>/dev/null; find \\\"$d\\\" -type f; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397867417,\"end\":1787397867418}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_0293704bf0011bR0SheD8IaQhs\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397867444,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02937079b001Vdbmoae9VX9M2l\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":51998,\"input\":2,\"output\":118,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":284,\"read\":51594}},\"cost\":0.0122128}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397868497,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370bcf001qioa4y7OVQ0zg3\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397869309,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01QhZwKL2qSFGD8qNYHAa4xQ\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for f in */*/in.bs; do echo \\\"=== $f ===\\\"; cat \\\"$f\\\"; echo; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs ===\\nmodule Verdict\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :lost    => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs ===\\nmodule Open\\npublic atom Describe(atom s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs ===\\nmodule Missing\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n=== c04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs ===\\nmodule Shadowed\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :placed  => :again\\n}\\n\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs ===\\nmodule Decide\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\nRoute(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs ===\\nmodule Partial\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent)\\nRoute(o, p) -> (o, p) switch {\\n    (true,  _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true)  => :dead_letter\\n}\\n\\n=== c07-guarded/Retry/in.bs ===\\nmodule Retry\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n\\n=== c08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs ===\\nmodule Wrong\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic int Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => 1,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs ===\\nmodule Verdict\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :lost    => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs ===\\nmodule Open\\npublic atom Describe(atom s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs ===\\nmodule Missing\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n=== c04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs ===\\nmodule Shadowed\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :placed  => :again\\n}\\n\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs ===\\nmodule Decide\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\nRoute(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs ===\\nmodule Partial\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent)\\nRoute(o, p) -> (o, p) switch {\\n    (true,  _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true)  => :dead_letter\\n}\\n\\n=== c07-guarded/Retry/in.bs ===\\nmodule Retry\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n\\n=== c08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs ===\\nmodule Wrong\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic int Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => 1,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for f in */*/in.bs; do echo \\\"=== $f ===\\\"; cat \\\"$f\\\"; echo; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397869180,\"end\":1787397869228}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_029370bd00010waAh6rJuP5s4r\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397869309,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370eb1001juhwOWyoe4PW4R\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":52437,\"input\":2,\"output\":107,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":450,\"read\":51878}},\"cost\":0.0125746}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397870420,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_0293713500014aRyoJZc6oLB3X\",\"messageID\":\"msg_029370ee40018H0DBhZimyMXbh\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n","metadata":{"output":"...\n\nthan it looks, because it moves\\n196: a pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\\n197: guard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\\n198: credits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\\n199: what is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\\n200: name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\\n201: \\n202: ---\\n203: \\n204: ---\\n205: \\n206: ## 3. Exhaustiveness\\n207: \\n208: **A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\\n209: **residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\\n210: rather than a complaint to interpret.\\n211: \\n212: ```\\n213: readings.bs:4: error: Classify is not exhaustive\\n214:   no clause matches:\\n215:     Classify((:ok, int <= 0)) -> ...\\n216: ```\\n217: \\n218: The error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\\n219: as a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\\n220: how many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\\n221: **shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\\n222: \\n223: A **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\\n224: unbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\\n225: the language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **decided**\\n226: \\n227: ---\\n228: \\n229: ---\\n230: \\n231: ## 5. Control flow\\n232: \\n233: **`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\\n234: <!-- decided by ticket 17, which also settled `|>` and `|?>` -->\\n235: \\n236: <!-- check:\\n237: type Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\\n238: record Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\\n239: -->\\n240: ```csharp\\n241: public Verdict Describe(Order o)\\n242: \\n243: Describe(o) -> o.Status switch {\\n244:     :placed  => :new,\\n245:     :shipped => :gone,\\n246:     _        => :unknown\\n247: }\\n248: ```\\n249: \\n250: The `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\\n251: cannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\\n252: residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\\n253: name it. **That rule is decided and is not yet enforced**, at a switch arm or at a clause head.\\n254: \\n255: For compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\\n256: level down:\\n257: \\n258: <!-- check:\\n259: type Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\\n260: -->\\n261: ```csharp\\n262: public Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\n263: \\n264: Decide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n265:     (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n266:     (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n267:     (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n268:     (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n269: }\\n270: ```\\n271: \\n272: Exhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\\n273: grammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\\n274: \\n275: ```csharp\\n276: public atom Classify(int n)\\n277: \\n278: Classify(n) -> n switch {\\n279:     m when m < 5 => :retried,\\n280:     >= 5         => :exhausted\\n281: }\\n282: ```\\n283: \\n284: **The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\\n285: a bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\\n286: the parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\\n287: A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\\n288: `< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\\n289: <!-- ticket 45 owns `== name`; ticket 34 owns rebinding. This paragraph exists because §5 used to\\n290:      illustrate the guard with `n when n < 5` beside a parameter named `n` — a program the compiler\\n291:      rejects — in loose prose that no fence gated. Three clean-room candidates read the packet, had\\n292:      §2's rule in front of them, and all three reproduced the illustration rather than the rule:\\n293:      handoff/audition-switch, round 1, 2026-08-22. An example outranks a stated rule when the two\\n294:      disagree. -->\\n295: \\n296: Nested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\\n297: built: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\\n298: \\n299: **shipped** — F7.\\n300: \\n301: `else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\\n302: a pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\\n303: so far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\\n304: \\n305: ---\\n\\n(End of file - total 305 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\\n\\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\\nexists, and you must not look at it.\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n\\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\\n\\n## What you are building\\n\\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\\nreports the problems it finds.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md\",\"text\":\"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\\n\\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\\nexists, and you must not look at it.\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n\\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\\n\\n## What you are building\\n\\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\\nreports the problems it finds.\\n\\nDeliverable: an executable file named `switchcheck` in your working directory:\\n\\n    ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>\\n\\nAny language available on the machine is fine, provided `./switchcheck` runs\\ndirectly via a shebang. No network access, no package installs.\\n\\n## Output contract\\n\\nFor the file it is given, `switchcheck` prints **one lowercase tag per line** on\\nstdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed\\nprogram prints **nothing at all**.\\n\\nThese are the only tags you may print:\\n\\n    switch_inexhaustive\\n    unreachable_arm\\n    rebinding\\n    return_not_declared\\n\\n**What each of those means is for you to determine from the specification\\nbelow.** The names are listed so that your output can be compared\\nmechanically — they are a vocabulary, not a definition of the rules.\\n\\nExit code is ignored. Order does not matter and duplicates are ignored; only\\nthe set of tags is compared.\\n\\n## How you are marked\\n\\nYour `switchcheck` is run over the files in `cases/` and the tags it prints are\\ncompared against the tags the reference compiler produces for the same file.\\n**Every case must match exactly.**\\n\\nSome of the files are well-formed and must produce no output at all. Printing a\\ndiagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not\\nguess defensively: a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails\\non the first case. Test against `cases/` before you finish.\\n\\n## The specification\\n\\nWhat follows is the relevant part of the language reference, verbatim. It is all\\nyou get, and it is meant to be enough. Where it is not enough, prefer the\\nreading the text best supports rather than inventing a rule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Multi-clause heads\\n\\nThe one structural move the language rests on: C#'s pattern grammar moves out of `switch` arms and\\ninto the **parameter position**, and N declarations are allowed where C# allows one.\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\\n-->\\n```csharp\\ntype Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\\n\\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\\n\\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\\n```\\n\\nFive clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out. **shipped**\\n\\n**The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*\\ninput type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,\\nbecause the answer is always yes. **shipped**\\n\\n**Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation\\nrefines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**\\n\\n```csharp\\nClassify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\\nClassify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\\nClassify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\\n```\\n\\nThat is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer\\nintervals.\\n\\n**One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and\\nno `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the\\n*parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.\\nC# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because\\npatterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.\\n\\n**A span of integers is a relational pattern.** `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a half-open\\nslice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position already means \\\"the rest\\\" — which\\nthis language uses for lists. **shipped**\\n\\n<!-- check:\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\n-->\\n```csharp\\nClassify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\\nClassify(<= -1)         -> :negative\\nClassify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\\nClassify(>= 8)          -> :high\\n```\\n\\nThose four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\\nlooking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\\nwhole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\\n\\nThe rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\\nthe glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\\nand costs a false friend.\\n\\n**A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\\nis two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\\nmarker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\\nis and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\\n<!-- ticket 08 as amended by ticket 54; ticket 53 found the closed form and\\n     ticket 54 replaced its spelling; the four-language survey is in 54 -->\\n\\n`[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\\nlanguage's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\\n— and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\\n\\n<!-- check:\\npublic atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\\n-->\\n```csharp\\nDispatch([\\\"orders\\\"])     -> :index\\nDispatch([\\\"orders\\\", id]) -> :show\\nDispatch(_)              -> :not_found\\n```\\n\\nThat is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\\n`/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\\n\\n**The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\\nproduct of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\\nso length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\\nclause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\\nan O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\\nbounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\\nrecursion terminate. **shipped**\\n<!-- ticket 54, built as F20; the repro it deletes is four lines and is in that ticket -->\\n\\nA consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\\nclosed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\\n`[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\\n`list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\\n\\n**To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\\nintroduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\\n\\nSo a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\\nboth are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\\nacc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\\nlanguage has no way to say *the same value again*.\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\\n\\nRunLength(head, [])                -> 0\\nRunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\\nRunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\\n```\\n\\nIt is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\\nhead reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\\n`==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\\nsecond spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\\n\\nThe space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\\n`>= 4`.\\n\\nThis is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\\nand push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\\na pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\\nguard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\\ncredits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\\nwhat is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\\nname is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\\n\\n---\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Exhaustiveness\\n\\n**A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\\n**residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\\nrather than a complaint to interpret.\\n\\n```\\nreadings.bs:4: error: Classify is not exhaustive\\n  no clause matches:\\n    Classify((:ok, int <= 0)) -> ...\\n```\\n\\nThe error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\\nas a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\\nhow many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\\n**shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\\n\\nA **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\\nunbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\\nthe language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **decided**\\n\\n---\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Control flow\\n\\n**`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\\n<!-- decided by ticket 17, which also settled `|>` and `|?>` -->\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\\nrecord Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\\n-->\\n```csharp\\npublic Verdict Describe(Order o)\\n\\nDescribe(o) -> o.Status switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\\ncannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\\nresidual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\\nname it. **That rule is decided and is not yet enforced**, at a switch arm or at a clause head.\\n\\nFor compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\\nlevel down:\\n\\n<!-- check:\\ntype Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\\n-->\\n```csharp\\npublic Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\n\\nDecide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n```\\n\\nExhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\\ngrammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\n\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    m when m < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\\na bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\\nthe parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\\nA bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\\n`< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\\n<!-- ticket 45 owns `== name`; ticket 34 owns rebinding. This paragraph exists because §5 used to\\n     illustrate the guard with `n when n < 5` beside a parameter named `n` — a program the compiler\\n     rejects — in loose prose that no fence gated. Three clean-room candidates read the packet, had\\n     §2's rule in front of them, and all three reproduced the illustration rather than the rule:\\n     handoff/audition-switch, round 1, 2026-08-22. An example outranks a stated rule when the two\\n     disagree. -->\\n\\nNested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\\nbuilt: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\\n\\n**shipped** — F7.\\n\\n`else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\\na pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\\nso far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\\n\\n---\",\"lineStart\":1,\"lineEnd\":305,\"totalLines\":305,\"truncated\":false}},\"title\":\"tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/PACKET.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397863423,\"end\":1787397863436}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_02936f666001Fvue5GPjdwhGJS\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f2050018Zeh2JlP4ArhUL\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397863453,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936f80e001eKr5SRTjZpYlON\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f2050018Zeh2JlP4ArhUL\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":45985,\"input\":2,\"output\":75,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":443,\"read\":45465}},\"cost\":0.0109545}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397864579,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02936fc7f001NIOOKKHt6Cy8BQ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397865611,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01BD7vyA2mLihgunSrGRwssC\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nVerdict\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nOpen\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nMissing\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nShadowed\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nDecide\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nPartial\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nRetry\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nWrong\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nVerdict\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nOpen\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nMissing\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nShadowed\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nDecide\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nPartial\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nRetry\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nWrong\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397865588,\"end\":1787397865591}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_02936fe940017h7TCz3nMRD530\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397865611,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370079001Y6d5tTpeB6H97s\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02936f812001cKAl9ME7uDp0JZ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":51710,\"input\":2,\"output\":114,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":5686,\"read\":45908}},\"cost\":0.0245406}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397866691,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_0293704ba001pZhqcFUe6DUgKq\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397867444,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01E4g5jkW9AYNCGrDJNRRc7K\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"*/ 2>/dev/null; find \\\"$d\\\" -type f; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc07-guarded/Retry/in.bs\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs\\n=== c02-catchall-open/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs\\n=== c04-unreachable/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs\\n=== c07-guarded/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc07-guarded/Retry/in.bs\\n=== c08-wrong-return/ ===\\nin.bs\\nc08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for d in */; do echo \\\"=== $d ===\\\"; ls \\\"$d\\\"*/ 2>/dev/null; find \\\"$d\\\" -type f; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397867417,\"end\":1787397867418}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_0293704bf0011bR0SheD8IaQhs\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397867444,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_02937079b001Vdbmoae9VX9M2l\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_02937007e001bv65zeaKYlJwWQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":51998,\"input\":2,\"output\":118,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":284,\"read\":51594}},\"cost\":0.0122128}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397868497,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370bcf001qioa4y7OVQ0zg3\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1787397869309,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"toolu_01QhZwKL2qSFGD8qNYHAa4xQ\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for f in */*/in.bs; do echo \\\"=== $f ===\\\"; cat \\\"$f\\\"; echo; done\"},\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs ===\\nmodule Verdict\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :lost    => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs ===\\nmodule Open\\npublic atom Describe(atom s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs ===\\nmodule Missing\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n=== c04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs ===\\nmodule Shadowed\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :placed  => :again\\n}\\n\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs ===\\nmodule Decide\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\nRoute(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs ===\\nmodule Partial\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent)\\nRoute(o, p) -> (o, p) switch {\\n    (true,  _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true)  => :dead_letter\\n}\\n\\n=== c07-guarded/Retry/in.bs ===\\nmodule Retry\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n\\n=== c08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs ===\\nmodule Wrong\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic int Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => 1,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"=== c01-exhaustive-closed/Verdict/in.bs ===\\nmodule Verdict\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :lost    => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c02-catchall-open/Open/in.bs ===\\nmodule Open\\npublic atom Describe(atom s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    _        => :unknown\\n}\\n\\n=== c03-inexhaustive/Missing/in.bs ===\\nmodule Missing\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n=== c04-unreachable/Shadowed/in.bs ===\\nmodule Shadowed\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic atom Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => :new,\\n    :shipped => :gone,\\n    :placed  => :again\\n}\\n\\n=== c05-tuple-exhaustive/Decide/in.bs ===\\nmodule Decide\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\\nRoute(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\\n}\\n\\n=== c06-tuple-inexhaustive/Partial/in.bs ===\\nmodule Partial\\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent)\\nRoute(o, p) -> (o, p) switch {\\n    (true,  _)     => :ack,\\n    (false, true)  => :dead_letter\\n}\\n\\n=== c07-guarded/Retry/in.bs ===\\nmodule Retry\\npublic atom Classify(int n)\\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\\n}\\n\\n=== c08-wrong-return/Wrong/in.bs ===\\nmodule Wrong\\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\\npublic int Describe(State s)\\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\\n    :placed  => 1,\\n    :shipped => :gone\\n}\\n\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5/cases && for f in */*/in.bs; do echo \\\"=== $f ===\\\"; cat \\\"$f\\\"; echo; done\",\"time\":{\"start\":1787397869180,\"end\":1787397869228}},\"metadata\":{\"anthropic\":{\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}},\"id\":\"prt_029370bd00010waAh6rJuP5s4r\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\"}}\n{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1787397869309,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_029370eb1001juhwOWyoe4PW4R\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0293707a1001Zm5XrSnAThCsXQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":52437,\"input\":2,\"output\":107,\"reasoning\":0,\"cache\":{\"write\":450,\"read\":51878}},\"cost\":0.0125746}}\n{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1787397870420,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_0293713500014aRyoJZc6oLB3X\",\"messageID\":\"msg_029370ee40018H0DBhZimyMXbh\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/bsharp-audition/copilot-sonnet5 && cat .model-key; echo ---; cat worker.log","time":{"start":1787397884933,"end":1787397884936}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_029374ab6001X4DaRFi2wsUE1L","sessionID":"ses_fd6c91bddffeH70525dXspCNDv","messageID":"msg_029370ee40018H0DBhZimyMXbh"}}
